# Maintainer batch 20260820-082516. NO ROWS.
#
# Seven parked tasks. No row can release them. A queue request cannot release them
# either. The program already names the owner tool that can, and that tool is not this
# batch.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. LJ-1.386, LJ-1.388, LJ-1.390, LJ-1.391, LJ-1.395. reason `no-match`.
#
# Each stored record is green, `error_class` null, `obligations_delta` 0, and it
# carries no `obligations_open`. The records:
#
#   c-27  LJ-1.386  exit 0, delta 0, 5 files   (corpus line 2)
#   c-122 LJ-1.388  exit 0, delta 0, 1 file    (corpus line 13)
#   c-80  LJ-1.390  exit 0, delta 0, 1 file    (corpus line 7)
#   c-116 LJ-1.391  exit 0, delta 0, 2 files   (corpus line 12)
#   c-128 LJ-1.395  exit 0, delta 0, 17 files  (corpus line 14)
#
# `sys-obligations-satisfied` (`dev/pod/table.toml:748-762`) keys on
# `obligations_open_max = 0`. `matches()` at `scripts/pod/table.py:563-566`
# returns false when that fact is absent. The last batch said so and it is
# still true.
#
# A TASK-SCOPED `done` ROW WOULD ADMIT UNDER R3: each of those five corpus
# records is currently NO MATCH, and a record that did not match MAY become a
# match (`scripts/pod/replay.py:17-18`, `:327`). I am not writing those rows.
# Rule (a2) at `scripts/pod/pod.py:3125-3134` sends a record-carrying park to
# READY when a row matches, so the five would be dispatched again. The program
# already measured that cost at `scripts/pod/pod.py:4314-4318`: re-running them
# burns agent time to buy a bookkeeping close. One of the five, LJ-1.391, is a
# critic return, not a coder close, so a `done` / `go` row for the group would
# also pick an outcome the facts do not name.
#
# THE LAST BATCH SAID THE REFILL WOULD DISPOSE OF THEM. That premise is false.
# Rule (a1) creates tasks from `dev/pod/queue.toml`. It does not un-park a
# PARKED code (`scripts/pod/pod.py:3058-3069`). These five have stayed parked
# since 2026-08-19.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. LJ-1.396, LJ-1.397. reason `no-change`.
#
# Both returned at 2026-08-19T08:54:13Z with `why: "pid dead"` and parked with
# no record (`dev/pod/transitions/2026-08.jsonl` seq 153 and 155). Their newest
# `*-final.md` files carry the vendor 429. MEASURED this batch:
#
#   .venv/bin/python, `vendor_refusal("LJ-1.396")`
#     -> 2026-08-19T20:19:47+08:00
#   .venv/bin/python, `vendor_refusal("LJ-1.397")`
#     -> 2026-08-19T20:19:47+08:00
#
# So `_no_change_reason()` would now stamp `quota:`. It did not, because these
# two parked at 08:54Z, before that stamp existed. LJ-1.398 and LJ-1.399 parked
# at 13:54Z with `quota:2026-08-19T20:19:47` (seq 161 and 163). The detector
# landed between the two pairs.
#
# A `no-change` park carries no record (`scripts/pod/pod.py:867-875`). Rule
# (a2) at `:3125-3126` then holds it for ever. A `[[queue]]` request with the
# same code is skipped, because `code in st.tasks` is already true
# (`scripts/pod/pod.py:3068-3069`). Section 6.7's brief-repair path is for a
# malformed brief. These two briefs are not malformed. The workers hit a
# vendor quota.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Expiry fallout, section 4.6. NO ROW.
#
# c-86, c-111, c-133 and c-138 were won by task rows of LJ-1.392, LJ-1.393 and
# LJ-1.394. Those tasks closed. `expire_rows()` retired the rows. Fallout to
# NO MATCH is the design (`dev/memos/LJ-4-pod-program-design.md:1278-1283`):
# expiry moves every record that row used to win, and R3 does not forbid a row
# from dying with its task. A system row that re-matched them would invent a
# policy the closed briefs already carried as task branches.
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# WHAT WAITS, AND IT IS NOT A ROW.
#
# `.pod-state/STOPPED` is present. `state.json` carries
# `"stopped": "2026-08-20T00:25:46Z"`. Seven parks is `parked_max`, so a plain
# `pod.py resume` un-parks none of them and rule (d) stops again on the first
# tick. That measurement is already in `scripts/pod/pod.py:4310-4313`.
#
# `pod.py resume --retry` is the owner's, for that resume
# (`dev/pod/instructions/maintainer.md:74-76`). The program already splits the
# seven: the quota pair never ran; the other five already delivered
# (`scripts/pod/pod.py:4314-4319`).

[batch]
ts = "20260820-082516"
rows_proposed = 0
reason = "No row can release the seven parks. The five no-match records predate fact 8, so sys-obligations-satisfied cannot reach them, and a task-scoped done row would re-dispatch them. The two no-change parks are a vendor quota frozen under the old reason; they carry no record, so a2 cannot un-park them and a same-code queue request is skipped. Expiry fallout is the closed tasks dying with their rows. resume --retry is the owner's."
